# Everything below except the two API variables is read on the server at request time,
# so a prebuilt image is configured entirely by the environment it starts in - no
# rebuild, and no value baked into anything the browser downloads. The older
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed spelling of each name still works as a fallback, for builds
# that already set it; the unprefixed name wins where both are present.

# -------- Where the API is --------
#
# Two variables, and which one you need depends on whether the browser talks to the API
# directly or through this app.
#
# A deployed instance uses neither. Unset, the browser calls `/api/v1` on the same origin
# that served the page, and the catch-all route handler at `app/api/v1/[...path]/route.ts`
# forwards each request to the API container. That is what lets one prebuilt image run on
# any domain with no rebuild - and it means no CORS to configure and no cross-site cookie
# to lose.

# The API container's address, read by that route handler on every request. An *origin*,
# with no `/api/v1` on the end: the request's own path is appended to it unchanged, so a
# prefix here would be doubled. The default is the compose service name, which is why the
# shipped bundle sets nothing; `next dev` falls back to `http://localhost:8000` instead.
# API_INTERNAL_URL=http://api:8000

# Worth knowing before this container answers on a public address: the forwarding chain
# the API sees is whatever reached *here*. This app copies `X-Forwarded-For` through
# unchanged - a route handler cannot see the socket peer, so it can pass on what a caller
# sent but never append to it. Whether the API believes that chain is decided by
# `TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS`, an API variable set in the API's own `.env`, and it should cover
# this container only when a proxy that *appends* `X-Forwarded-For` - Caddy, Traefik,
# nginx - really sits in front of it. That is the shipped shape, and there the appended
# entry is the real caller, so anything a browser put in front of it is ignored. Serve
# this container directly with nothing in front and list it anyway, and the reverse holds:
# the caller supplies the only entry in the chain, the API takes it, and every per-IP rate
# limit the API has - magic-link requests and the contact form among them - is bypassable
# by changing one header. Leaving it unset costs one shared rate-limit bucket for
# everybody, which is the cheaper of the two mistakes.

# The browser's own API base, and the one variable here that is genuinely baked into the
# bundle at build time - `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is inlined by the compiler where it is written as
# a literal, so changing it means rebuilding. Set it only for a split-origin deployment,
# where the API answers on a host of its own and the proxy route above is bypassed
# entirely. `src/proxy.ts` reduces it to an origin for the CSP's `connect-src`, so the
# path is harmless there.
#
# Local development is the everyday case for setting it: this points the browser straight
# at the API's own published port, which is how the stack has always run, and
# `scripts/screenshots.mjs` reads the same variable from Node, where an absolute URL is
# the only kind that works.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1

# Example split-origin production value (uncomment and modify)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.opendiving.app/api/v1

# -------- This instance --------

# The origin this instance is reached at, used for the `og:`/`twitter:` tags and for
# resolving relative URLs in page metadata. Nothing functional depends on it - get it
# wrong and link previews point at the wrong host, that is all - but the default is
# `http://localhost:3000`, which is nobody's public address, so set it. An absolute
# origin: scheme and host, no trailing path.
# SITE_URL=https://dives.example.com

# Optional. Address offered on /contact as a fallback if a form submission fails.
# Display only - the form posts to the API, which decides where the message actually
# goes (CONTACT_FORM_EMAIL there); setting this does not change delivery. Leave it
# unset unless it matches that address: a failed submission then points at the GitHub
# issue tracker, which is never the wrong advice.
# CONTACT_EMAIL=contact@opendiving.app

# Google Sign-In (OAuth 2.0 client ID from the Google Cloud Console, "Web application"
# type). Not a secret - safe to expose to the browser, and the only half of the pair
# this app ever holds: the client secret belongs to the API, which is what redeems the
# authorization code. Leave unset to hide the "Continue with Google" button.
#
# Nothing of Google's is loaded into the browser either way. Pressing the button
# navigates to Google; until then this app makes no request to any Google address, so
# `accounts.google.com` appears in no CSP directive whether this is set or not. The
# button is the only contact there is, which is also why turning this on discloses
# nothing about a visitor who does not use it.
#
# Must match the API's GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, and the API's GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET must be set
# alongside it or the API refuses to start.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# -------- Map tiles (the dive site dialog's "Pick on a map") --------
# Raster tile templates, in `{z}/{x}/{y}` form. Both default to OpenStreetMap's own
# standard tiles: keyless, no watermark, and - as of August 2026 - the only basemap
# still answering an unregistered request from any domain with place names on it.
#
# OSM has no dark variant, so in the dark theme the app inverts these tiles itself.
# Configure MAP_TILE_URL_DARK and that stops: a provider's real dark tiles are always
# better than a filter, so setting one switches the filter off.
#
# The host is the one and only thing the map adds to the CSP - it's derived from these
# variables into `img-src` by `src/proxy.ts`, so a tile host hardcoded anywhere else is
# blocked rather than merely misconfigured. Give a plain host with no `{s}` subdomain
# placeholder: sharding is an HTTP/1.1 workaround, and one fixed host is one exact CSP
# source instead of a wildcard.
# MAP_TILE_URL=https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
# Falls back to MAP_TILE_URL when unset - so pointing the light one at your own tile
# server and leaving this alone means "use mine", not "use mine by day and a
# stranger's by night".
# MAP_TILE_URL_DARK=
# Rendered over the map. For OpenStreetMap-derived tiles, crediting is a licence
# condition and their tile policy asks for it plainly visible - change this whenever
# you change the tile URLs above. `[label](url)` becomes a link, so the credit can
# point at the licence rather than just naming it; anything outside that syntax renders
# as plain text, and a link that isn't http(s) keeps its label and loses its href.
# MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION="[© OpenStreetMap contributors](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright)"

# Two optional placeholders, for a provider that is better than the keyless default.
#
# `{key}` is replaced by MAP_TILE_API_KEY, so the credential is written once instead of
# pasted into both URLs. Every provider spells the parameter differently, which is why
# the query string belongs to your template: CARTO wants `?key=`, Stadia `?api_key=`.
#
# `{r}` marks where a provider's high-density variant goes - empty for the plain tile,
# `@2x` for the one carrying four times the pixels. Both are offered to the browser,
# which fetches whichever suits the display, so a retina screen gets sharp labels rather
# than an upscaled 256 px image. Spell it only where the variant exists and you're
# entitled to it: OSM serves none, and CARTO's is watermarked without a key.
#
# CARTO's Positron and Dark Matter - a matched light/dark pair, which is what the app
# shipped with until CARTO began watermarking keyless tiles. A key is free (5M tiles a
# month, no account, emailed back from carto.com/basemaps/apikey), and with one the
# `{r}` retina tiles are worth turning on:
# MAP_TILE_URL=https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/light_all/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png?key={key}
# MAP_TILE_URL_DARK=https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png?key={key}
# MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION="[© OpenStreetMap contributors](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright) [© CARTO](https://carto.com/attributions)"
# MAP_TILE_API_KEY=
#
# Stadia's Alidade Smooth and Alidade Smooth Dark - the same kind of matched pair, with
# a clean `@2x`. A key needs an account (stadiamaps.com), and the free tier is tighter
# than CARTO's on every count: 200k tiles a month against 5M, non-commercial use only,
# and a hard 429 for the rest of the month once it's spent (paid plans from $20/month).
# What it buys is a raster product with a future - CARTO recommends its vector basemaps
# and may stop updating the raster data, while Stadia maintains raster as a first-class
# offering, and this app's map only speaks raster:
# MAP_TILE_URL=https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png?api_key={key}
# MAP_TILE_URL_DARK=https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth_dark/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png?api_key={key}
# MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION="[© Stadia Maps](https://stadiamaps.com/) [© OpenMapTiles](https://openmaptiles.org/) [© OpenStreetMap contributors](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright)"
# MAP_TILE_API_KEY=
#
# The key reaches the browser, because the browser is what fetches the tiles - there is
# no way around that for any client-side map. Treat it as a public, domain-restricted
# credential rather than a secret, and restrict it at the provider.

# Worth knowing before you leave the default in place: tiles are fetched by the browser,
# so opening the picker tells the tile host your users' IP addresses and the z/x/y of the
# area they're looking at - which, for a dive log, is roughly where their sites are. It's
# the tile coordinates only; `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` (set in
# `next.config.js`) keeps the site UUID out of the `Referer` header. Nothing else about
# the app leaves the machine. If that trade doesn't suit your instance, point these at
# your own tile server - it's the one place self-hosting buys real privacy here, and the
# CSP follows the variable automatically.

# -------- Serving this instance --------

# `Strict-Transport-Security`, on by default and sent only for a request that already
# arrived over HTTPS - so an instance behind a TLS-terminating proxy gets it and one on a
# plain-HTTP LAN address never does, without either of them having to say so. It does not
# include `preload`: entering a domain into the browsers' preload list commits every host
# under it to HTTPS for years and is deliberately hard to undo, which is a choice for
# whoever owns the domain. Set this to `off` if a proxy in front should own the header, or
# if a browser must be able to reach this instance over HTTP after seeing it over HTTPS.
# WEB_HSTS=off

# Keeps search engines out: `robots.txt` disallows everything and every page is served
# with `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow`. Off by default. Worth setting on a private
# instance that is nonetheless reachable from the internet - a `Disallow` alone does not
# stop a URL somebody linked from being listed, which is why both go together.
# WEB_NOINDEX=true
